Notebook[{
Cell["The Layers Behind a Graph Force Network", "Title"],
Cell["Every Wolfram Language layer used in PolymerNN, explained on a four-bead polymer", "Subtitle"],
Cell["\:00a9 2026 Computational Classnotes \:00b7 CC BY-NC-SA 4.0
Authors: Dara O Shayda \:00b7 Noor Hoshino", "Text"],
Cell["The force network in PolymerNN.wl is built from a handful of standard layers: LinearLayer, ElementwiseLayer, NetChain, NetMapOperator, ThreadingLayer, ReplicateLayer, NetArrayLayer and DotLayer. They are joined in a NetGraph through NetPorts and trained with NetTrain. This notebook takes them one at a time. Each one is applied to a tiny chain of four beads, small enough that every number can be checked by hand, and each layer is compared with the same computation written in ordinary Wolfram Language.", "Text"],
Cell["By the end, the pieces are assembled into a working mini force network, which is trained to learn a spring force from examples. It is the polymer network in miniature. This notebook does not need PolymerNN.wl.", "Text"],
Cell["Grid[Prepend[{
   {\"LinearLayer\", \"W.x + b\", \"the three linear maps inside phiB and phiN\"},
   {\"ElementwiseLayer[Tanh]\", \"tanh of each entry\", \"the nonlinearity inside phiB and phiN\"},
   {\"NetChain\", \"layers one after another\", \"phiB and phiN themselves\"},
   {\"NetMapOperator\", \"the same net on every row\", \"applies phi to every edge\"},
   {\"ThreadingLayer[Times]\", \"entrywise product\", \"cut (phi times cutoff), mB and mN (f times u)\"},
   {\"ThreadingLayer[Plus]\", \"entrywise sum\", \"total (bond + non-bonded forces)\"},
   {\"ReplicateLayer[3, 2]\", \"copy each number 3 times\", \"repB and repN\"},
   {\"NetArrayLayer\", \"a stored array, optionally frozen\", \"Ab and An, the incidence matrices\"},
   {\"DotLayer\", \"matrix product\", \"sumB and sumN (A.m)\"},
   {\"NetGraph, NetPort\", \"wiring with named inputs and outputs\", \"the whole force network\"}},
  Style[#, Bold] & /@ {\"layer\", \"what it computes\", \"where PolymerNN uses it\"}], Frame -> All, Alignment -> Left, Background -> {None, {LightBlue}}]", "Input"],
Cell["1. The example: a chain of four beads", "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{"Four beads, three bonds (1-2, 2-3, 3-4) and three non-bonded pairs (1-3, 1-4, 2-4). These are the edges of the graph. For each edge we need its length r and its unit vector u = (", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["x", "i"]]], " - ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["x", "j"]]], ")/r."}], "Text"],
Cell["x4 = {{0., 0., 0.}, {1., 0., 0.}, {0.55, 0.88, 0.}, {-0.25, 1.3, 0.5}};
bonds = {{1, 2}, {2, 3}, {3, 4}};
pairs = {{1, 3}, {1, 4}, {2, 4}};
edgeGeometry[x_, pr_] := Module[{d = x[[pr[[All, 1]]]] - x[[pr[[All, 2]]]], r},
   r = Sqrt[Total[d^2, {2}]]; {r, d/r}];
{rB, uB} = edgeGeometry[x4, bonds];
{rN, uN} = edgeGeometry[x4, pairs];
Grid[Prepend[Join[Transpose[{bonds, rB, uB}], Transpose[{pairs, rN, uN}]], {\"edge\", \"r\", \"u\"}], Frame -> All]", "Input"],
Cell["Graphics3D[{{Gray, Tube[x4, 0.04]}, {Orange, Dashed, Line[x4[[#]]] & /@ pairs},
  {RGBColor[0.2, 0.4, 0.8], Sphere[x4, 0.12]}, MapIndexed[Text[Style[First[#2], Bold, 16], #1 + {0, 0, 0.25}] &, x4]},
 Boxed -> False, PlotLabel -> \"bonds (grey) and non-bonded pairs (orange)\"]", "Input"],
Cell[TextData[{"The networks never see r directly. They see a radial basis, the vector of Gaussians ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03c1", "k"]]], "(r) = exp(-\:03b3 (r - ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03bc", "k"]]], Cell[BoxData[SuperscriptBox[")", "2"]]], "). Here there are four centres ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["\:03bc", "k"]]], ", so each edge becomes a 4-vector, and the 3 bonds give a 3\:00d74 matrix. PolymerNN uses 16 centres. The smooth cutoff c(r) is 1 at r = 0 and falls to 0 at ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["r", "c"]]], ". Here ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["r", "c"]]], " = 2, so all three pairs feel it (PolymerNN uses ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["r", "c"]]], " = 1.5)."}], "Text"],
Cell["mu = {0.8, 1.0, 1.2, 1.4}; gw = 25.;
rbf[r_] := Exp[-gw Outer[Subtract, r, mu]^2];
cutoff[r_] := UnitStep[2. - r] 0.5 (Cos[Pi r/2.] + 1);
bfeat = rbf[rB]; nfeat = rbf[rN]; nc = cutoff[rN];
{MatrixForm[bfeat], MatrixForm[nfeat], nc}", "Input"],
Cell["2. LinearLayer", "Section"],
Cell["LinearLayer[n] computes W.x + b with an n\:00d7d weight matrix W and a bias vector b of length n. Normally NetInitialize or NetTrain chooses the weights at random. Here we set them by hand, so the result can be checked against W.x + b.", "Text"],
Cell["W1 = {{0.5, -1., 0.2, 0.}, {0.1, 0.3, -0.4, 0.8}}; b1 = {0.05, -0.1};
lin = LinearLayer[2, \"Weights\" -> W1, \"Biases\" -> b1, \"Input\" -> 4]", "Input"],
Cell["{lin[bfeat[[1]]], W1.bfeat[[1]] + b1}", "Input"],
Cell["Like every layer, a LinearLayer accepts a batch: a list of inputs gives a list of outputs.", "Text"],
Cell["lin[bfeat] - Map[W1.# + b1 &, bfeat] // Chop", "Input"],
Cell["LinearLayer[{}] gives a scalar, not a length-1 vector. It is the last layer of phi, which returns one pair force per edge. Its weights are a 1\:00d7d matrix.", "Text"],
Cell["lin0 = LinearLayer[{}, \"Weights\" -> {{1.5, -0.7}}, \"Biases\" -> {0.2}, \"Input\" -> 2];
{lin0[{1., 2.}], 1.5*1. - 0.7*2. + 0.2}", "Input"],
Cell["3. ElementwiseLayer", "Section"],
Cell["ElementwiseLayer[f] applies f to every entry. Without it the network would be linear, however many layers it had. PolymerNN uses Tanh, which is smooth, so the learned forces are smooth functions of r.", "Text"],
Cell["tanh = ElementwiseLayer[Tanh];
{tanh[{-2., 0., 0.5, 3.}], Tanh[{-2., 0., 0.5, 3.}]}", "Input"],
Cell["4. NetChain", "Section"],
Cell["NetChain puts layers one after another. Here it is a one-hidden-layer version of phi: radial basis (4) \:2192 linear (2) \:2192 tanh \:2192 linear (scalar). PolymerNN's phi has two hidden layers of width 32: 16 \:2192 32 \:2192 tanh \:2192 32 \:2192 tanh \:2192 1.", "Text"],
Cell["phi = NetChain[{lin, ElementwiseLayer[Tanh], lin0}]", "Input"],
Cell["phiByHand[v_] := First[{{1.5, -0.7}}.Tanh[W1.v + b1] + {0.2}];
{phi[bfeat[[1]]], phiByHand[bfeat[[1]]]}", "Input"],
Cell["5. NetMapOperator", "Section"],
Cell["phi takes one edge, a 4-vector. The chain has several edges, a P\:00d74 matrix. NetMapOperator[phi] applies phi to every row with the same weights, giving one number per edge. This weight sharing is what makes the trained network independent of the chain length: the number of parameters does not depend on P.", "Text"],
Cell["phiMap = NetMapOperator[phi];
{phiMap[bfeat], phiByHand /@ bfeat}", "Input"],
Cell["{\"parameters in phi\" -> Information[phi, \"ArraysTotalElementCount\"],
 \"parameters in NetMapOperator[phi], 3 edges\" -> Information[NetMapOperator[phi, \"Input\" -> {3, 4}], \"ArraysTotalElementCount\"],
 \"parameters in NetMapOperator[phi], 465 edges\" -> Information[NetMapOperator[phi, \"Input\" -> {465, 4}], \"ArraysTotalElementCount\"]}", "Input"],
Cell["The scalar on each edge is a pair force f(r). A positive value pushes the two beads apart.", "Text"],
Cell["fB = phiMap[bfeat]; fN = phiMap[nfeat];
{fB, fN}", "Input"],
Cell["6. ThreadingLayer", "Section"],
Cell["ThreadingLayer[g] combines several arrays entry by entry with g. Its inputs are ports Input1, Input2, and so on; given a list {a, b}, the first goes to Input1 and the second to Input2. PolymerNN uses it three times with Times and once with Plus.", "Text"],
Cell[TextData[{"Times, first use (cut): the non-bonded pair force is multiplied by the cutoff, so it fades smoothly to zero at ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["r", "c"]]], "."}], "Text"],
Cell["times = ThreadingLayer[Times];
{times[{fN, nc}], fN nc}", "Input"],
Cell["Plus (total): the bond forces and the non-bonded forces on each bead are added.", "Text"],
Cell["plus = ThreadingLayer[Plus];
a = {{1., 2., 3.}, {4., 5., 6.}}; b = {{10., 20., 30.}, {40., 50., 60.}};
{plus[{a, b}], a + b}", "Input"],
Cell["7. ReplicateLayer", "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{"A force is a 3-vector, but phi gives a scalar per edge. To multiply each scalar by its unit vector u (P\:00d73), the scalar vector (length P) is first copied into a P\:00d73 array. ReplicateLayer[3, 2] inserts a new level 2 of size 3, so row p becomes {", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["f", "p"]]], ", ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["f", "p"]]], ", ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["f", "p"]]], "}."}], "Text"],
Cell["rep = ReplicateLayer[3, 2];
{MatrixForm[rep[fB]], MatrixForm[Transpose[{fB, fB, fB}]]}", "Input"],
Cell["Compare level 1, the default: ReplicateLayer[3] makes three copies of the whole vector (3\:00d7P), which is not what we want.", "Text"],
Cell["{Dimensions[ReplicateLayer[3][fB]], Dimensions[ReplicateLayer[3, 2][fB]]}", "Input"],
Cell["8. The message on every edge", "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{"Times, second use (mB, mN): the replicated scalars are multiplied by the unit vectors, giving the force vector ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["m", "p"]]], " = ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["f", "p"]]], " ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["u", "p"]]], " that edge p exerts on its first bead."}], "Text"],
Cell["mB = times[{rep[fB], uB}];
{MatrixForm[mB], MatrixForm[fB uB]}", "Input"],
Cell["9. NetArrayLayer", "Section"],
Cell["NetArrayLayer stores an array inside the network. It has no input; its output is the array. If the array is a learnable parameter, it is trained like a weight. Setting LearningRateMultipliers -> 0 freezes it, so it is constant data that training cannot change. PolymerNN stores the incidence matrices this way.", "Text"],
Cell["The incidence matrix A of the bonds has one row per bead and one column per edge. Column p has +1 at the first bead of edge p, -1 at the second, and 0 elsewhere.", "Text"],
Cell["incidence[n_, pr_] := Normal@SparseArray[
    Flatten[MapIndexed[{{#1[[1]], #2[[1]]} -> 1., {#1[[2]], #2[[1]]} -> -1.} &, pr], 1], {n, Length[pr]}];
Ab = incidence[4, bonds]; An = incidence[4, pairs];
{MatrixForm[Ab], MatrixForm[An]}", "Input"],
Cell["arrB = NetArrayLayer[\"Array\" -> Ab, LearningRateMultipliers -> 0];
{arrB[] == Ab, Dimensions[arrB[]]}", "Input"],
Cell["10. DotLayer", "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{"DotLayer[] is the matrix product of its two inputs. A.m adds every edge message to its two beads: +", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["m", "p"]]], " to the first and -", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["m", "p"]]], " to the second, which is Newton's third law. The result is the force on each bead, a 4\:00d73 array."}], "Text"],
Cell["dot = DotLayer[];
FB = dot[{Ab, mB}];
{MatrixForm[FB], MatrixForm[Ab.mB]}", "Input"],
Cell[TextData[{"Every column of A sums to zero, so the forces on the chain always add up to zero, whatever phi has learned. (Networks compute in 32-bit floats, so zero means about ", Cell[BoxData[SuperscriptBox["10", "-7"]]], ", hence the Chop.)"}], "Text"],
Cell["{Total[Ab], Chop[Total[FB], 10^-6]}", "Input"],
Cell["11. NetGraph and NetPort: assembling the force network", "Section"],
Cell["A NetGraph names its layers and lists the connections. NetPort[\"name\"] is an input or output of the whole graph. {a, b} -> c sends a to Input1 and b to Input2 of c. This is the bond branch of PolymerNN's network for our four-bead chain: inputs bf (3\:00d74) and bu (3\:00d73), output F (4\:00d73).", "Text"],
Cell["bondNet = NetGraph[<|
   \"phiB\" -> NetMapOperator[phi], \"repB\" -> ReplicateLayer[3, 2], \"mB\" -> ThreadingLayer[Times],
   \"Ab\" -> NetArrayLayer[\"Array\" -> Ab, LearningRateMultipliers -> 0], \"sumB\" -> DotLayer[]|>,
  {NetPort[\"bf\"] -> \"phiB\" -> \"repB\", {\"repB\", NetPort[\"bu\"]} -> \"mB\", {\"Ab\", \"mB\"} -> \"sumB\" -> NetPort[\"F\"]},
  \"bf\" -> {3, 4}, \"bu\" -> {3, 3}]", "Input"],
Cell["{bondNet[<|\"bf\" -> bfeat, \"bu\" -> uB|>] - FB // Chop}", "Input"],
Cell["The full network adds the non-bonded branch, with the cutoff, and a Plus at the end. Apart from the sizes, this is exactly BuildForceNet in PolymerNN.wl.", "Text"],
Cell["forceNet[phiBond_, phiPair_] := NetGraph[<|
    \"phiB\" -> NetMapOperator[phiBond], \"phiN\" -> NetMapOperator[phiPair], \"cut\" -> ThreadingLayer[Times],
    \"repB\" -> ReplicateLayer[3, 2], \"repN\" -> ReplicateLayer[3, 2],
    \"mB\" -> ThreadingLayer[Times], \"mN\" -> ThreadingLayer[Times],
    \"Ab\" -> NetArrayLayer[\"Array\" -> Ab, LearningRateMultipliers -> 0],
    \"An\" -> NetArrayLayer[\"Array\" -> An, LearningRateMultipliers -> 0],
    \"sumB\" -> DotLayer[], \"sumN\" -> DotLayer[], \"total\" -> ThreadingLayer[Plus]|>,
   {NetPort[\"bf\"] -> \"phiB\" -> \"repB\", {\"repB\", NetPort[\"bu\"]} -> \"mB\", {\"Ab\", \"mB\"} -> \"sumB\",
    NetPort[\"nf\"] -> \"phiN\", {\"phiN\", NetPort[\"nc\"]} -> \"cut\" -> \"repN\",
    {\"repN\", NetPort[\"nu\"]} -> \"mN\", {\"An\", \"mN\"} -> \"sumN\", {\"sumB\", \"sumN\"} -> \"total\" -> NetPort[\"F\"]},
   \"bf\" -> {3, 4}, \"bu\" -> {3, 3}, \"nf\" -> {3, 4}, \"nu\" -> {3, 3}, \"nc\" -> 3];
fullNet = forceNet[phi, phi]", "Input"],
Cell["F4 = fullNet[<|\"bf\" -> bfeat, \"bu\" -> uB, \"nf\" -> nfeat, \"nu\" -> uN, \"nc\" -> nc|>];
byHand = Ab.(fB uB) + An.((fN nc) uN);
{MatrixForm[F4], Max[Abs[F4 - byHand]], Chop[Total[F4], 10^-6]}", "Input"],
Cell["Information looks inside a network: its ports, its layers and its parameter count. NetExtract takes out a single layer.", "Text"],
Cell["{Information[fullNet, \"InputPorts\"], Information[fullNet, \"OutputPorts\"],
 Information[fullNet, \"ArraysTotalElementCount\"], NetExtract[fullNet, \"cut\"]}", "Input"],
Cell["12. NetTrain: learning a spring", "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{"Now the bond branch learns a force from examples. The examples are chains of four beads joined by harmonic springs, U = (k/2)(r - ", Cell[BoxData[SuperscriptBox["1)", "2"]]], " with k = 50, so the exact pair force is f(r) = -50 (r - 1). The bond lengths are drawn around 1 and the forces on the beads are computed exactly. NetTrain gets only the bead forces, never f(r) itself; it has to work out the pair force behind them. This is force matching, as in PolymerNN."}], "Text"],
Cell["kSpring = 50.;
randomChain[] := Accumulate[Prepend[Table[RandomReal[NormalDistribution[1., 0.12]] Normalize[RandomVariate[NormalDistribution[], 3]], {3}], {0., 0., 0.}]];
springForces[x_] := Module[{r, u}, {r, u} = edgeGeometry[x, bonds]; Ab.((-kSpring (r - 1.)) u)];
mu8 = N@Subdivide[0.6, 1.4, 7]; gw8 = 1/(mu8[[2]] - mu8[[1]])^2;
rbf8[r_] := Exp[-gw8 Outer[Subtract, r, mu8]^2];
SeedRandom[4]; chains = Table[randomChain[], {3000}];
data = <|\"bf\" -> (rbf8[First[edgeGeometry[#, bonds]]] & /@ chains), \"bu\" -> (Last[edgeGeometry[#, bonds]] & /@ chains),
   \"F\" -> (springForces /@ chains)|>;
Dimensions /@ data", "Input"],
Cell["The network to train: the bond branch again, now with 8 basis functions and phi = 8 \:2192 16 \:2192 tanh \:2192 1, starting from random weights.", "Text"],
Cell["springNet = NetGraph[<|
   \"phiB\" -> NetMapOperator[NetChain[{LinearLayer[16], ElementwiseLayer[Tanh], LinearLayer[{}]}]],
   \"repB\" -> ReplicateLayer[3, 2], \"mB\" -> ThreadingLayer[Times],
   \"Ab\" -> NetArrayLayer[\"Array\" -> Ab, LearningRateMultipliers -> 0], \"sumB\" -> DotLayer[]|>,
  {NetPort[\"bf\"] -> \"phiB\" -> \"repB\", {\"repB\", NetPort[\"bu\"]} -> \"mB\", {\"Ab\", \"mB\"} -> \"sumB\" -> NetPort[\"F\"]},
  \"bf\" -> {3, 8}, \"bu\" -> {3, 3}];
SeedRandom[5];
res = NetTrain[springNet, data, All, ValidationSet -> Scaled[0.1], BatchSize -> 64, LearningRate -> 0.01,
   MaxTrainingRounds -> 400, TimeGoal -> 30, TrainingProgressReporting -> None];
trained = res[\"TrainedNet\"];
{\"rounds\" -> res[\"TotalRounds\"], \"RMS force error\" -> Sqrt[res[\"FinalValidationLoss\"]],
 \"RMS force\" -> Sqrt[Mean[Flatten[data[\"F\"]^2]]]}", "Input"],
Cell["The trained phi is a function of one variable, so it can be drawn and compared with the exact spring force -50 (r - 1). The shaded band is the range of bond lengths in the data; outside it the network has no information.", "Text"],
Cell["phiLearned = NetExtract[trained, {\"phiB\", \"Net\"}];
rr = Subdivide[0.6, 1.4, 200]; rTrain = Flatten[First[edgeGeometry[#, bonds]] & /@ chains];
Show[Plot[-kSpring (r - 1), {r, 0.6, 1.4}, PlotStyle -> {Dashed, Black}],
 ListLinePlot[Transpose[{rr, phiLearned[rbf8[rr]]}], PlotStyle -> {Thick, RGBColor[\"#E07B00\"]}],
 Graphics[{Opacity[0.12], Blue, Rectangle[{Quantile[rTrain, 0.005], -25}, {Quantile[rTrain, 0.995], 25}]}],
 PlotRange -> {-25, 25}, Frame -> True, FrameLabel -> {\"r\", \"pair force f(r)\"},
 PlotLabel -> \"learned (orange) vs exact spring (dashed)\", ImageSize -> 500]", "Input"],
Cell["The frozen NetArrayLayer really was frozen: training left the incidence matrix unchanged.", "Text"],
Cell["{Normal[NetExtract[trained, {\"Ab\", \"Array\"}]] == Ab, Normal[NetExtract[trained, {\"Ab\", \"Array\"}]] // MatrixForm}", "Input"],
Cell["13. From the toy to the polymer", "Section"],
Cell["Every layer of PolymerNN's network has now appeared. The polymer version differs only in size and in the data:
\:2022 16 basis functions instead of 4 or 8, and phi = 16 \:2192 32 \:2192 tanh \:2192 32 \:2192 tanh \:2192 1.
\:2022 For N = 32 beads: 31 bonds and 465 non-bonded pairs instead of 3 and 3.
\:2022 The reference forces come from Langevin simulations of a bead-spring chain with WCA repulsion, not from random springs.
\:2022 A fixed repulsive core is added, and the input distance is clamped to the range of the data. Section 12 shows why: outside the shaded band the network is guessing.
In PolymerNN.wl, BuildForceNet builds the same graph as forceNet above, and ForceNetGraphPlot draws it.", "Text"]
}, StyleDefinitions -> "Default.nb"]
